**Reasoning:**
- The move-based hill climb often gets stuck when every candidate destination GPU lacks free memory for any model on the worst GPU, or when moving any model would raise another GPU's KVPR above the current max.
- Swapping models between the worst-KVPR GPU and others preserves total memory usage on both GPUs' constraints (checked via size-difference vs. free memory) and can rebalance pressure without needing spare capacity.
- The swap pass only runs when all moves fail, so the execution time overhead is bounded and the algorithm remains well within the time limit (execution time is a tiny part of the combined score anyway, dominated by max_kvpr).
- Complexity remains modest: O(iterations × models² × gpus), fine for typical test sizes.
